      "latency_ms": 19138.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1120,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant bonding is the source of almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Uniquely, ice is **less dense than liquid water** (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has a very high **surface tension** and **specific heat capacity**.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), facilitating chemical reactions and nutrient transport.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** It takes a lot of energy to heat water up. This stabilizes Earth’s climate (oceans absorb heat by day/release by night) and regulates organism body temperatures.\n*   **High Heat of Vaporization:** Evaporation requires massive energy, making sweating/panting highly effective cooling mechanisms.\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Liquid water is densest at 4°C. As it cools further toward freezing, it expands. This causes **turnover** in lakes, oxygenating deep water.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Allows capillary action (water moving up plant roots/stems) and supports small insects (water striders).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium for Metabolism:** Almost all biochemical reactions (hydrolysis, condensation, photosynthesis, respiration) occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports nutrients, oxygen, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structural Support:** Turgor pressure in plant cells (water pushing against cell walls) keeps plants rigid.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** High heat capacity and evaporative cooling maintain homeostasis.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, of which:\n    *   ~68.7% is locked in ice caps/glaciers.\n    *   ~30.1% is groundwater.\n    *   **< 1.2% is surface water** (lakes, rivers, swamps) — the primary source for human use.\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Challenges\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Municipal/Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Challenges:** Scarcity (physical/economic), pollution (industrial, agricultural, plastic), climate change altering precipitation patterns, and access inequality (SDG 6: Clean Water and Sanitation).\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a polar inorganic compound that is a liquid at standard temperature and pressure. Its unique hydrogen-bonded structure gives it anomalous properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, solvent ability, and a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase—that make it the indispensable solvent and structural agent for terrestrial life and the primary driver of Earth's climate system.**"
